export default "Top / point: The spot straight out from the rim, at the top of the key above the 3-point line (heard as: top of the key, point, top of key, the key)\nSlot: Where the lane line, if you extended it, meets the 3-point line, between the top and the wing (heard as: slots, right slot, left slot, guard spot, the lot, slaw)\nWing: The 3-point line at about free-throw-line height, on the side (heard as: wings, right wing, left wing, wink, ring)\n45 / 45 spot: Wing-to-corner area at a 45° angle to the rim; also the angle a corner player cuts on (heard as: forty-five, four five, the 45, for tea five)\nCorner: Where the 3-point line meets the baseline, the shortest three (heard as: corners, right corner, deep corner, coroner)\nShort corner: Baseline area between the lane and the corner, inside the arc (heard as: short corner, sport corner, baseline short)\nDunker spot: Just outside the lane on the baseline, next to the rim, where a big waits for a dump-off (heard as: dunker, dunkers spot, dunk spot, donker, drunk her spot)\nBlock / low post: The marks on the lane line closest to the rim (heard as: blocks, low block, low post, the lock)\nElbow: Where the free-throw line meets the lane line (heard as: elbows, high post corner, el bow, elbo)\nHigh post / free-throw line: The middle of the free-throw line area (heard as: high post, foul line, FT line, free throw)\nNail: The middle of the free-throw line, the classic help spot for a defender one pass away (heard as: the nail, nale, knell)\nPaint / lane: The rectangle under the basket (heard as: paint, lane, key, the pain)\nLift: An off-ball player moves up (e.g. corner to wing) to give the driver room and a passing angle (heard as: lift up, lift it, left)\nDrift: When the ball drives baseline, the weak-side wing slides down to the corner (heard as: drift down, drift to the corner, drip, trift)\nFill / fill behind: Replace the spot a driver or cutter just left (heard as: fill, fill behind, fill the spot, feel, phil)\nSpacing: Stay 12–15 ft apart so one defender can't guard two (heard as: space, spread out, spread the floor, facing)\n5-out: All five players on the perimeter, nobody posted inside (heard as: five out, 5 out, five down, fight out)\n4-out-1-in: Four on the perimeter, one big in the post (heard as: four out one in, 4 out 1 in, for out won in)\nBall side / help side: The half of the floor the ball is on / the other half (heard as: strong side, weak side, help side, helpside)\nEmpty side: A side of the floor with no offensive player, to give a ball screen or post room (heard as: clear out, cleared side, empty sight)\nBasket cut: Cut straight to the rim after passing (pass and cut) (heard as: basket cut, cut to the basket, bask it cut)\nGive-and-go: Pass, cut, get it right back (heard as: give and go, give n go, give an' go)\nBackdoor: When the defender denies the pass, cut behind them to the rim (heard as: back door, back-door, back cut, black door)\nV-cut: Walk the defender in, then plant and pop out to get open (heard as: v cut, vee cut, be cut, we cut)\nL-cut: Walk up the lane, then cut sharply out to the wing (heard as: l cut, el cut, elk cut)\n45 cut: Corner player cuts toward the rim at an angle when their defender helps on a drive (heard as: forty-five cut, 45 cut, slash)\nFlash: Weak-side player cuts hard into the lane/high post to show for the ball (heard as: flash, flash to the middle, flash middle, flesh)\nDown screen / pin-down: Screener sets a screen facing the baseline so a teammate can come up to the ball (heard as: pin down, pindown, down screen, pin town)\nBack screen: Screen set behind a defender (screener's back to the rim) so a teammate cuts to the basket (heard as: back screen, backscreen, bad screen)\nFlare screen: Screen that lets a shooter fade away from the ball for a shot (heard as: flare, flare screen, flair, player screen)\nCross screen: Screen across the lane, usually block to block, to free a post (heard as: cross screen, across screen, cross scream)\nUCLA cut: Passer cuts to the rim off a back screen from the high post (Wooden's high-post offense) (heard as: UCLA, U C L A, UCLA screen, you see LA)\nFlex: Baseline (flex) screen for a cutter across the lane, then screen-the-screener down screen (heard as: flex cut, flex screen, flex offense, flecks)\nScreen the screener (STS): After setting a screen, the screener gets screened right away (heard as: screen the screener, STS, screen to screener)\nStagger: Two screens in a row on the same side for one cutter (heard as: staggered, stagger screen, double stagger, swagger)\nFloppy: Shooter starts under the rim with a single screen on one side and a stagger/double on the other; chooses one (heard as: floppy, flop, sloppy, floppy set)\nZipper: Player at the block cuts straight up to the top off a down screen (heard as: zipper, zipper cut, zip, zip her)\nRam: Off-ball screen *for the ball screener*, right before they set a ball screen (screener gets screened) (heard as: ram, ram screen, ran, lamb)\nIverson cut: Wing cuts across the floor above two elbow screeners (horns) to catch at the other wing (heard as: Iverson, Iverson cut, I-version, eye verse in)\nChicago: Pin-down that flows straight into a dribble handoff (DHO) (heard as: Chicago, Chicago action, Chi-town, she cargo)\nZoom: Same idea as Chicago (pin-down into DHO); many teams use Zoom when it starts from the elbow/block (heard as: zoom, zoom action, elbow zoom, boom, zoo)\nGhost screen: Screener runs at the ball like a screen but never makes contact, then pops or slips (heard as: ghost, ghost screen, goes screen, post screen)\nSlip: Screener cuts to the rim before contact because the defense jumps the screen (heard as: slip, slip screen, slip it, flip)\nShake: When a teammate drives, the player near them moves to a new open spot (often baseline) (heard as: shake, shake up, shape)\nRelocate: After passing (or when a teammate drives), move to an open spot behind the defense's eyes (heard as: relocate, re-locate, real low kate)\nPick-and-roll (PnR): Ball screen, screener rolls to the rim (heard as: pick and roll, PNR, P N R, ball screen, pick in roll)\nPick-and-pop: Ball screen, screener steps out for a shot (heard as: pick and pop, pop, pick and pot)\nDrag screen: Ball screen in transition, before the defense is set (heard as: drag, drag screen, double drag, drug screen)\nDouble drag / 77: Two trailers set back-to-back drag screens in transition; one rolls, one pops (heard as: double drag, 77, seventy-seven, seven seven)\nSpain PnR: Top pick-and-roll where a third player back-screens the roller's defender, then pops (heard as: Spain, Spain pick and roll, stack PnR, spane, pain)\nHorns: 1-2-2 set: bigs on both elbows, shooters in the corners; ball handler can use either ball screen (heard as: horns, horn set, horn, corns, harness)\nHorns twist: Two consecutive ball screens from the two elbow bigs; handler changes direction (heard as: horns twist, twist, horns quist)\nHorns flare: From horns, one big sets a flare screen for a shooter while the other ball-screens (heard as: horns flare, flare out of horns)\nDelay / elbow: Big catches at the top/elbow and becomes the passer; guards cut backdoor or come get a handoff (heard as: delay, elbow, big at the elbow, the lay, dee lay)\nDHO / get: Dribble handoff: dribbler hands the ball to a teammate running past, like a screen with the ball (heard as: DHO, D H O, dribble handoff, handoff, hand off, get, the H O)\nPistol / 21: Early offense: point guard and wing (1 and 2) play a quick give-and-go/handoff on one side, then a big screens (heard as: pistol, 21, twenty one, two one, pisstol)\nRip: A UCLA-style back screen / cut off the high post; also used for a quick cut past the high post (heard as: rip, rip cut, rip screen, trip)\nStep-up screen: Ball screen set on the side, stepping up from below the ball (often early offense) (heard as: step up, step-up screen, set up)\nEmpty / side PnR: Pick-and-roll on a side with no teammate in the corner (heard as: empty corner, side pick and roll)\nPost entry: Pass into a post player who seals the defender (heard as: post entry, feed the post, throw it in, post pantry)\nSeal: Post player pins the defender behind them with body/arm and shows a target hand (heard as: seal, seal it, steal)\nTriple threat: Catch facing the rim, ready to shoot, pass, or dribble (heard as: triple threat, triple-threat, triple thread)\nJump stop: Land on two feet at once so you can use either foot as a pivot (heard as: jump stop, two-foot stop, jumps top)\nThree-lane break: Ball in the middle, two runners wide, rim to rim (heard as: three lanes, 3-lane, fast break, free lane)\nRim run: First big sprints straight to the rim in transition (heard as: rim run, rim runner, run the rim, rim rum)\nTrailer: The last player up the floor, often for a drag screen or a three (heard as: trailer, trail, tailor)\nEarly offense: Quick action off a made/missed basket before the defense sets (drag, pistol) (heard as: early, early offense, secondary break)\nOutlet: First pass after the rebound to a guard on the side (heard as: outlet, outlet pass, out let)\nSprint back: Defense runs back past the ball before guarding (heard as: get back, sprint back, spring back)\nPress break: Getting the ball up the floor vs. full-court pressure; 1-4 / 4 across alignment, flash to the ball, middle then long (heard as: press break, press breaker, break the press, press brake, one four)\nMan-to-man: Each defender guards one player (heard as: man to man, man, player-to-player, man and man)\nHelp / gap / nail: Off-ball defenders sag toward the ball in the gaps and the nail, ready to stop a drive (heard as: help defense, help side, gap, sink, hell defense)\nSee ball, see man: Off-ball stance with vision on both the ball and your player (heard as: ball you man, pistols, see both, sea ball)\nJump to the ball: On every pass, all defenders move toward where the ball went (heard as: jump to the ball, jump to ball, jump the ball)\nDeny: One-pass-away defender takes away the passing lane (heard as: deny, denial, one pass away, the night)\nCloseout: Run at a shooter, then chop your steps with hands high (heard as: close out, closeout, clothes out)\nTag: Weak-side defender bumps the roller to slow them, then recovers (heard as: tag, tag the roller, tack)\nX-out: Rotation where two weak-side defenders swap to cover a skip pass (heard as: x out, ex out, eggs out)\nRotate: When a teammate helps, the next defender covers for them (heard as: rotate, rotation, help the helper)\nICE / down / blue: Vs a side ball screen, on-ball defender jumps to the top and forces the dribbler to the sideline/baseline; big sits low (heard as: ice, ice it, down, blue, push, eyes, ice it down)\nDrop: Screener's defender sags back to the paint to protect the rim (heard as: drop, drop coverage, sag, drip)\nHedge / show: Screener's defender jumps out in front of the dribbler briefly, then recovers (heard as: hedge, show, hard hedge, edge, head)\nSwitch: Defenders swap players on a screen (heard as: switch, switch it, swish)\nBlitz / trap: Both defenders trap the ball handler off the screen (heard as: blitz, trap, double, blitzed)\nOver / under: Fight over the top of a screen vs. go under it (heard as: over, under, go over, go under)\nBox out / hit and get: On the shot, find a player, make contact (hit), then go get the ball (heard as: box out, boxout, block out, hit and get, box doubt)\n2-3 zone: Two defenders up top, three across the back, guarding areas not players (heard as: two three zone, 2 3, zone, too three)\nFull-court press: Defend the inbound and the whole floor, often trapping (heard as: press, full court press, dress)\nPass and cut: Never stand after a pass; cut to the rim\nFill the empty spot: Replace the spot someone left\nTriple threat: Catch facing the rim\nJump to the ball: Move on every pass, on defense\nTwo big steps apart: Spacing\nHands up / hand target: Show where you want the pass\nBall! Ball! Ball!: On-ball defender's talk\nHelp! I got ball!: Helper calls the stop\nShot!: Everyone boxes out\nWall up: Vertical arms vs a driver at the rim\nWide and still: Screen cue: wide base, don't move\nShoulder to shoulder: Dribbler uses the screen tightly";
